  Every change is one you just made, one second ago, looking right at the screen. A notification telling you about it is
  noise. The value in that sentence — "tasks [3mthey[0m care about change" — comes from [3msomeone else[0m doing the changing, and
  there's nobody else in this app.

  Second thing: there are no tasks. [38;5;153m<main>[39m is empty. There's no task model, no storage, no list, no rendering. So
  "notifications for task changes" is a subsystem with nothing underneath it.

  So I don't think you should build a notifications system right now, and I'd rather say that than design one that's
  technically correct and practically useless. Here's what I think the real options are:

  [1mThe fork:[0m what would actually be worth notifying about in a single-user browser app?

  - [1mTime-based reminders[0m — "Task due in 1 hour." This is the one kind of notification that keeps its value with a single
    user, because the trigger is the clock, not another person. Costs: tasks need due dates and real persistence; and
    to fire while the tab is closed you need the browser Notification API plus a service worker plus an OS permission
    prompt. In-page only, it's much cheaper but only works while the tab is open.
  - [1mSince-last-visit digest[0m — "3 tasks changed since you were here." Only has value if changes arrive from somewhere you
    weren't watching. Across two open tabs that's real (localStorage/BroadcastChannel). Across devices it isn't,
    without a backend. Narrow payoff.
  - [1mActivity feed with undo[0m — a running log of recent changes you can reverse. Not really notifications, but it's the
    thing that's genuinely useful when you're the only one touching the data. Cheap.
  - [1mBuild the task list first, with one change choke point[0m — all mutations flow through a single store/event seam. No
    notifications yet, but any of the three above attaches later without a rewrite, and the seam costs nothing extra to
    build in from the start.

  [1mMy recommendation: the last one, then reminders later.[0m The seam is the part that's expensive to retrofit and cheap to
  design in now; the notification flavor is the part that's cheap to change once you know which one you want. Building
  tasks first also means you'll actually have opinions about the notification question in a week, instead of guessing at
  it today.

  That said — building the task list is a bigger change to your request than I should make on my own. Which way do you
  want to go?

